# Plugin-Agnostic Neovim Colorscheme Development (Nvim 0.10–0.12)

## TL;DR

- A truly plugin-agnostic Neovim colorscheme is achievable: define a small, well-chosen set of "hub" highlight groups (`Normal`, `NormalFloat`, `FloatBorder`, `FloatTitle`, `Pmenu*`, `StatusLine*`, `WinBar*`, `WinSeparator`, `LineNr`, `CursorLine*`, `Visual`, `Search`, `CurSearch`, `MatchParen`, `Diagnostic*`, `Diff*`, `Added`/`Changed`/`Removed`, plus the standard syntax groups and the Treesitter `@`-capture root groups). Almost every well-behaved plugin (telescope, nvim-cmp, blink.cmp, gitsigns, neo-tree, lualine, trouble, noice, snacks, mini.*, nvim-tree, indent-blankline, flash, which-key, notify) declares its own groups with `default = true` and links them to those base groups, so they inherit your colors automatically.
- Use `vim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, name, opts)` exclusively (no `:hi clear`/Vimscript). Set `vim.g.colors_name`, set `vim.o.background`, and set `vim.g.terminal_color_0..15`. Do NOT pass `default = true` from a colorscheme — your definitions should win, and the plugin authors' `default = true` links will lose to yours, which is exactly the desired behaviour. The 0.10 default scheme broke the old "`hi clear` + assume defaults" pattern, so define every attribute you rely on explicitly.
- The Treesitter capture tree resolves via "shorten on dot" fallback (`@variable.parameter.builtin → @variable.parameter → @variable`), and LSP semantic tokens (`@lsp.type.*`, `@lsp.mod.*`, `@lsp.typemod.*.*`, optionally suffixed `.<filetype>`) cascade the same way. Neovim 0.9+ ships default links from `@lsp.type.<x>` to corresponding `@<x>`/standard groups, so if you cover the `@`-tree, semantic tokens "just work." Neovim 0.12 added an `update = true` option to `nvim_set_hl` for additive edits, and `vim.hl` (the renamed `vim.highlight`) exposes `vim.hl.priorities` (`syntax=50`, `treesitter=100`, `semantic_tokens=125`, `diagnostics=150`, `user=200`).

