## Details

### 1. The full `nvim_set_hl` API surface

Function signature: `vim.api.nvim_set_hl(ns_id, name, val)`.

- **`ns_id`**: `0` means "global highlights" (what `:colorscheme` and `:highlight` modify). Non-zero is a namespace created via `vim.api.nvim_create_namespace('foo')`. Namespace highlights are not active by default; activate with `vim.api.nvim_set_hl_ns(ns)` (global) or `vim.api.nvim_win_set_hl_ns(win, ns)` (per-window). This is how plugins like noice, treesitter highlighter, and built-in diagnostics scope their highlights without polluting global state. **A colorscheme should always use `ns_id = 0`.**

- **`name`**: any valid identifier. Treesitter capture groups are written as `"@variable.parameter"`. Hyphens cause `E5248` (filetype `foo-bar` therefore can't appear in `@lsp.type.X.foo-bar`).

- **`val` keys (Neovim 0.12)**:
  - `fg`, `bg`, `sp`: `"#RRGGBB"`, color name, `"NONE"`, or the special strings `"fg"`/`"bg"` (alias for `Normal`'s fg/bg).
  - `bg_indexed`, `fg_indexed`: bool; treat color as terminal palette index 0–255.
  - `blend`: 0–100 integer (alpha for floating windows when `winblend`/`pumblend` is in effect).
  - Style flags (boolean): `bold`, `italic`, `standout`, `reverse`, `underline`, `undercurl`, `underdouble`, `underdotted`, `underdashed`, `strikethrough`, `nocombine`, `altfont`, `dim` (0.12), `blink` (0.12), `conceal` (0.12), `overline` (0.12).
  - `cterm`: nested table mirroring the style flags, taking precedence in non-true-color terminals. E.g. `cterm = { bold = true, underline = true }`. Setting top-level style flags also sets them in cterm by default.
  - `ctermfg`, `ctermbg`: integer 0–255 or color name.
  - `link`: name of another group to link to; if set, all other attribute keys are *ignored* (matches `:highlight link` semantics).
  - `default`: bool; if `true` the call is a no-op when the group is already defined (matches `:highlight default`). **Plugins use this; colorschemes generally don't.**
  - `force`: bool; if `true`, overrides even if the group is currently linked elsewhere.
  - `update` (0.12): bool; if `true`, merge the supplied keys into the existing definition instead of replacing it. Lets you patch one attribute without a `get_hl` round-trip.

- **`vim.api.nvim_get_hl(ns_id, opts)`**: opts can include `name = "X"` to fetch one group, `id = 42` for an integer id, `link = false` to follow links and return resolved attributes. Returns the same dict shape as `nvim_set_hl` accepts. Use `create = false` (in 0.10+) to avoid implicitly creating an empty group when the name doesn't exist.

### 2. The fallback chain mechanics

Neovim resolves a highlight in this order of precedence at draw time, picking the **highest-priority defined** entry:

1. **Window-local namespace** (set via `nvim_win_set_hl_ns`) — exact group.
2. **Global namespace 0** — exact group.
3. **`link` chain** — if the resolved group's attributes are empty but it has a `link`, follow the link to its target, recursively. There is *no* automatic Treesitter dot-fallback for non-`@` groups; that fallback only applies inside the Treesitter highlighter when it consults captures.
4. For Treesitter captures specifically: the highlighter, when emitting an extmark for capture `@a.b.c`, resolves the longest defined prefix: tries `@a.b.c`, then `@a.b`, then `@a`. If none are defined, no highlight is set for that match. (See `runtime/lua/vim/treesitter/highlighter.lua`.) The same dot-shortening logic applies to LSP semantic-token group derivation.
5. Final attributes are composited: `fg`/`bg`/`sp` come from the highest-priority group with non-`NONE` values; style flags (`bold`, `italic`, etc.) accumulate by default unless `nocombine` is set on the higher-priority group, in which case it replaces lower-priority styles.

