### 9. Backwards compatibility considerations

- **0.10 minimum**: `WinBar`, `WinBarNC`, `Added`/`Changed`/`Removed`, `SnippetTabstop`, the `@markup.*` Treesitter renames. If you support 0.9 keep also defining the old `@text.*` aliases as links.
- **0.11 features**: `PmenuMatch`, `PmenuMatchSel`, `LspReferenceTarget`, `DiagnosticVirtualLines*`, `vim.hl.priorities`, `'winborder'`. Wrap anything 0.11-only in `if vim.fn.has('nvim-0.11') == 1 then ... end`.
- **0.12 features**: `update = true`, `DiffTextAdd`, `dim`/`blink`/`conceal`/`overline` SGR attrs, `'pumborder'`. The `update = true` option is a no-op on older Neovim — calls just replace as before, which is functionally fine for a colorscheme.

You can detect support via `vim.fn.has('nvim-0.10')`/`'nvim-0.11'`/`'nvim-0.12'`. Avoid using deprecated APIs like `nvim_get_hl_by_name` (deprecated in 0.9) or `nvim_get_hl_by_id`; use `nvim_get_hl(0, { name = ... })`.

### 10. Pitfalls and edge cases

- **`nvim_set_hl` after startup with empty namespaces**: There is a long-standing edge case where in a `--clean` session, calling `nvim_set_hl(0, 'CursorLine', {bg='#ff0000'})` after startup sometimes appears not to apply (issue #18266); using `vim.cmd('hi CursorLine guibg=#ff0000')` works. Modern Neovim (0.10+) has largely fixed this, but if you hit it, run highlights from a `VimEnter` autocmd or just inside a `colors/*.lua` file.
- **`default = true` and `:hi`** display: Neovim 0.10 has a cosmetic bug where `default = true` adds spurious empty `cterm=` `gui=` to `:hi` output (issue #23474). Harmless.
- **Hyphenated filetypes**: `@lsp.type.X.foo-bar` raises `E5248`. Workaround: don't suffix semantic token groups with the filetype, or rename the filetype. (issue #23184)
- **`@variable` no longer falls back to `Identifier` after 0.10**: define `@variable` explicitly.
- **`hi clear Folded` with link**: Folded historically rejected linking with `E414` in some cases. Always set `Folded` with full attributes, not just a link.
- **Terminal vs GUI**: If you support cterm-only terminals (linux console, no truecolor), populate `cterm`, `ctermfg`, `ctermbg` for every group — these don't auto-derive from gui values. Many themes skip this entirely; if true-color is a hard requirement, at least set `vim.o.termguicolors = true` from your `colors/*.lua` and document it.
- **Plugin hot-reload**: After `:colorscheme foo`, plugins listening to `ColorScheme` re-establish their `default` links. If your scheme dynamically changes `Normal`/`NormalFloat` after the initial load (e.g. transparency toggle), you'll need to re-emit `:doautocmd ColorScheme` (or `vim.api.nvim_exec_autocmds('ColorScheme', { pattern = vim.g.colors_name })`) so plugins refresh.

### 11. Recommended workflow

1. Build a Lua palette (`{ bg, bg_alt, fg, fg_alt, red, green, yellow, blue, magenta, cyan, gray, plus selection/diff/diagnostic accents }`).
2. Construct a *semantic* layer mapping palette colors to roles (`syntax.keyword`, `syntax.string`, `ui.border`, `ui.float_bg`, `diff.add`, `diag.error`, etc.). Don't reference raw hex from highlight definitions.
3. Define core editor groups, then standard syntax groups, then Treesitter `@` roots, then `Diagnostic*`, then `@lsp.*` overrides. Use `link` aggressively *within your scheme* to keep it DRY (e.g. `@function.method = { link = '@function' }`).
4. Set terminal colors.
5. Test with `:Inspect`, `:InspectTree`, `:checkhealth`, and the `:hi` command.
6. Test plugin coverage by spinning up a kitchen-sink config (LazyVim is a good benchmark) and visually verifying telescope, blink.cmp/nvim-cmp, gitsigns, neo-tree, lualine, trouble, noice, snacks pickers, mini.* — without ever writing a plugin-specific override.

